WEBVTT

00:00:00.314 --> 00:00:02.820
Strong validation starts with a clear contract.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:07.765
Before processing data, define what the application expects: type, length, format, range,

00:00:07.765 --> 00:00:11.710
required fields, optional fields, allowed values, nested objects, arrays,

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:12.860
and business rules.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:13.960
If the contract is vague,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.940
validation usually becomes a collection of partial checks that are hard to test and easy

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.380
to bypass accidentally.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.795
Type checks alone are not enough.

00:00:20.795 --> 00:00:23.680
A value may be a string but still be too long, in the wrong format,

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.940
or unacceptable for the current workflow.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.685
A number may parse successfully but exceed an allowed range.

00:00:28.685 --> 00:00:32.420
An array may contain the right general type but include too many items or unexpected

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:33.740
nested objects.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.660
A boolean may be valid syntactically but not a field the user is allowed to set.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:38.880
Business rules complete the contract.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.085
A start date should come before an end date.

00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:43.285
A role value should come from an approved set.

00:00:43.285 --> 00:00:47.815
An account identifier should match the authenticated user or tenant context.

00:00:47.815 --> 00:00:51.620
A price, discount, or workflow state should follow the application's rules.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.495
Well-formed data can still be unsafe when it violates business meaning.

00:00:55.495 --> 00:00:57.988
Contracts should be visible to developers, testers, and reviewers.

00:00:57.988 --> 00:01:01.730
Schema definitions, shared validation functions, API specifications,

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.460
TypeScript interfaces paired with runtime schemas, and documented request models all help.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.030
The more explicit the contract,

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:12.080
the easier it is to reject invalid input consistently and test the behavior when future

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:12.940
changes arrive.
